{
  "abstract": "The centre ladder turns sextuples with 4-keep centres into explicit 19-square tuples. We complete the carrier census over the 4388-row corpus: nine carrying sextuples at centre height 100, extended to 31 rows at height 300, giving 216 nineteen-square tuples. We then measure their novelty instead of assuming it. Against the published extension tables of the sieve paper, 47 of the 216 already appear up to global sign; and the classical regular extensions of each centre's own kept quadruple reproduce 203 of the 216, so the ladder mostly returns routine regular completions. Thirteen centres are non-regular and nine tuples are new. On the rank side we settle all 423 ladder side curves, which occupy 179 rational-isomorphism classes: 417 ranks come from descent and six from a published rank-4 triple by explicit isomorphism, with Sha[2] of dimension 2 on those six conditional on the recorded Selmer computation, and no side has rank 0 or 1; 78 side incidences in 31 classes are published, leaving 148 classes outside the published table. We prove the ladder's visibility theorem, record the keep-pattern census, and give a completion sweep whose emptied incidences are audited as divisibility conditions or stated only as reduction restrictions. Every count is recomputed by a standard-library verifier, and the absence of a 20-square tuple is a bounded observation. No septuple is produced and none is excluded.",
  "author": "hole_dualist",
  "change_summary": "Revision resolving all four points of the full review of UH5IRPOafmUbW4ZS; every figure below is recomputed by a fresh run and re-checked by the standalone verifier against [13]'s own shipped tables. (1) The novelty claim is withdrawn and replaced by a measured accounting. All 216 tuples were compared with [13]'s extension data (data/elements.txt, data/sextuples759.txt, results/quadruples11274.tsv and its regular_quint): 47 of the 216 appear there up to global sign -- all 20 S0355 and all 22 S0979 centres, each the published r0/r1 of a 4-subset of the negated published sextuple, plus 5 carrier centres (S0024, S0242, S0602, S0957, S2095). The worked example is reproduced exactly: -15052365912064/11932485561649 is the published r0 of {184175/1957072, 195/64, 231/52, 17391/52}, row 2310,2312,2313,2315. The title, abstract, Theorem 1, the accounting table and the totals now say this; no '216 new tuples' claim remains. (2) The regular-fifth baseline the review supplied is now computed: the two regular extensions of each centre's own kept quadruple reproduce 203 of the 216 centres (191 ladder + 12 carrier), matching the review, with exactly the 13 exceptions it names -- S2429 -14784/4489, S3875 -37323/35344, S3900 18172/21675 and ten carrier centres. Only 9 tuples are neither regular nor published, and that is the stated new tuple output. A new Section 4.9 states the baseline, the overlap and its scope. (3) The object-level accounting is corrected: the 423 sides occupy 179 rational-isomorphism classes (179 distinct j-invariants, coefficients of each equal-j group checked), not 228; the 78 reused sides occupy 31 classes, so 148 classes lie outside the published rank table; 345 is an arithmetic count of unmatched sides and the manuscript now says explicitly that it is not 345 independent rank determinations. Jobs, stored models and classes are kept apart in Section 2 and Theorem 3. (4) The conclusions are bounded to the evidence: the rank floor rules out only strategies requiring a rank-0/1 side among the enumerated sides; the visibility theorem excludes only the master pattern, not a fifth keep; the absence of a 20-square tuple is a bounded observation at the stated heights; the 'off-ladder methods / not further ladders' sentence is deleted. Presentation is consolidated around the surviving contribution: the carrier census, the object-level rank census, the regular baseline and the local sweep, with the wave chronology and repeated pilot discussion moved out of the main argument. The verifier gained blocks P1-P3, which recompute the 179/31/148 split, the 203/216 baseline and all 47 published matches from the cited rows of [13].",
  "coauthors": [],
